---
/**
 * CTXone editions — Team & Enterprise product descriptions.
 *
 * Replaces the former Early Access Program page (retired). This is a
 * product page, NOT a pricing page and NOT an application form: it
 * describes what the Team and Enterprise editions are, what they add
 * over the open core, and who each is for. Pricing is intentionally
 * absent. A single contact CTA (mailto) is the only action.
 */
import LandingLayout from '../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';

const contact = 'mailto:hello@agentstatelabs.com';
---

<LandingLayout
	title="CTXone Team & Enterprise — editions"
	description="CTXone Team gives your whole team one shared context hub. CTXone Enterprise adds multi-tenancy, SSO, RBAC, audit, and governance for org-wide agent deployments. Both build on the open-source core."
>
	<section class="section intro">
		<div class="container narrow">
			<div class="eyebrow">Editions</div>
			<h1>The same engine. Built for one, a team, or the whole org.</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				CTXone's open core is complete and self-hosted — every MCP tool,
				the full memory graph, plans, branches, taint, and provenance.
				<strong>Team</strong> and <strong>Enterprise</strong> don't unlock
				features; they run that same engine for many people, with the
				identity, sharing, and governance that a group needs.
			</p>
			<div class="tier-jump">
				<a href="#team">CTXone Team ↓</a>
				<a href="#enterprise">CTXone Enterprise ↓</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- TEAM -->
	<section id="team" class="section tier tier-team">
		<div class="container narrow">
			<div class="tier-head">
				<span class="tier-tag tag-team">TEAM</span>
				<h2>One hub your whole team points at.</h2>
			</div>
			<p class="tier-lede">
				Everyone runs against a single shared Hub, so context is shared by
				default — not copied, primed, or re-explained per person. What one
				engineer teaches CTXone, the whole team's agents already know.
			</p>

			<h3>What you get</h3>
			<ul class="feat">
				<li>
					<strong>A shared team Hub.</strong> One daemon, one graph, every
					tool and teammate connected to it by URL. No per-laptop database
					drifting out of sync.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Per-user identity.</strong> Commits, blame, and
					<code>why-did-we</code> attribute to real people — so a decision
					traces back to who actually made it, not an anonymous agent.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Branch workflows across contributors.</strong> Sandbox
					speculative context on a branch, review the diff, and merge it into
					the shared graph the same way you'd merge code.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Shared plans.</strong> Plans live on the team hub and
					survive across people and sessions, so work-in-flight is visible to
					everyone instead of trapped in one person's chat history.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Priority support.</strong> A direct line for setup, upgrades,
					and the occasional "why is recall ranking this?" question.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3>Who it's for</h3>
			<p>
				Teams already using AI coding tools daily who are tired of each
				member's agent starting from zero. If you've ever settled an
				architectural decision in Slack only to watch a teammate's model
				re-litigate it a month later, Team is the fix.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ENTERPRISE -->
	<section id="enterprise" class="section tier tier-ent">
		<div class="container narrow">
			<div class="tier-head">
				<span class="tier-tag tag-ent">ENTERPRISE</span>
				<h2>Agent context, governed at org scale.</h2>
			</div>
			<p class="tier-lede">
				Everything in Team, plus the multi-tenancy, access control, and
				auditability required to run CTXone across many teams in a regulated
				environment — without giving up the self-hosted, data-stays-put
				posture of the open core.
			</p>

			<h3>What Enterprise adds</h3>
			<ul class="feat">
				<li>
					<strong>Multi-tenancy.</strong> Isolate graphs by team, project, or
					business unit within one deployment, with clear boundaries between
					who can see and write what.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>RBAC.</strong> Role-based access control over paths and
					operations — read, write, branch, merge, administer — mapped to
					your org's roles.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>SSO / SAML.</strong> Authenticate through your existing
					identity provider. No separate CTXone accounts to provision or
					offboard.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Audit bundles.</strong> Exportable, tamper-evident records
					of who wrote, changed, or removed context and when — provenance you
					can hand to a compliance reviewer.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Policy governance.</strong> Codify verification gates
					(taint / quarantine) as org policy, so sensitive paths require
					review or are blocked from automated writes.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Data residency & compliance support.</strong> Deploy where
					your data is required to live, with support for HIPAA, SOC 2, and
					GDPR-aligned operating requirements.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Direct founder access & roadmap influence.</strong> A
					working relationship with the people building CTXone, and real
					input into what ships next.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3>Who it's for</h3>
			<p>
				Organizations in compliance-forward industries — healthcare,
				financial services, regulated public sector, large SaaS — deploying
				AI agents internally at scale, who need governance and auditability
				as a precondition, not an afterthought.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section contact">
		<div class="container narrow">
			<h2>Talk to us</h2>
			<p>
				Tell us what you're building and which edition fits. We'll walk you
				through deployment, identity, and governance for your environment.
			</p>
			<div class="contact-actions">
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${contact}?subject=CTXone%20Team`}>
					Contact us about Team →
				</a>
				<a class="btn btn-secondary" href={`${contact}?subject=CTXone%20Enterprise`}>
					Contact us about Enterprise →
				</a>
			</div>
			<p class="oss-note">
				Just getting started or evaluating solo? The
				<a href="/getting-started/quickstart/">open-source edition</a>
				is the same engine — install it and run it today, no account.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</LandingLayout>

<style>
	.narrow { max-width: 46rem; }

	.eyebrow {
		color: var(--ctx-accent);
		font-family: var(--ctx-font-mono);
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
	}

	h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.85rem);
		font-weight: 800;
		color: #ffffff;
		margin: 0 0 1.25rem 0;
		line-height: 1.12;
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
	}

	.lede {
		color: var(--ctx-text-dim);
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		line-height: 1.65;
		margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0;
	}

	.lede strong { color: #ffffff; }

	.tier-jump {
		display: flex;
		gap: 1rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.tier-jump a {
		font-family: var(--ctx-font-mono);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--ctx-border-hi);
		border-radius: var(--ctx-radius-md);
		color: var(--ctx-text);
	}

	.tier-jump a:hover { text-decoration: none; border-color: var(--ctx-accent); }

	.tier { position: relative; }

	.tier::before {
		content: '';
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		top: 0;
		bottom: 0;
		width: 3px;
	}

	.tier-team::before {
		background: var(--ctx-accent);
		box-shadow: 0 0 12px rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.5);
	}

	.tier-ent::before {
		background: var(--ctx-warn);
		box-shadow: 0 0 12px rgba(252, 211, 77, 0.5);
	}

	.tier-head {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.85rem;
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.tier-tag {
		font-family: var(--ctx-font-mono);
		font-size: 0.68rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
		border-radius: 999px;
	}

	.tag-team {
		background: rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.1);
		border: 1px solid rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.4);
		color: var(--ctx-accent-text);
	}

	.tag-ent {
		background: rgba(252, 211, 77, 0.1);
		border: 1px solid rgba(252, 211, 77, 0.4);
		color: var(--ctx-warn);
	}

	h2 {
		font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 1.9rem);
		color: #ffffff;
		margin: 0;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
	}

	h3 {
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.1em;
		color: var(--ctx-text-faint);
		font-family: var(--ctx-font-mono);
		margin: 2rem 0 1rem 0;
	}

	.tier-lede {
		color: var(--ctx-text);
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.65;
		margin: 0;
	}

	.feat {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
	}

	.feat li {
		position: relative;
		padding-left: 1.5rem;
		color: var(--ctx-text-dim);
		font-size: 0.98rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
		margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
	}

	.feat li::before {
		content: '→';
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		color: var(--ctx-text-faint);
	}

	.tier-team .feat li::before { color: var(--ctx-accent); }
	.tier-ent .feat li::before { color: var(--ctx-warn); }

	.feat strong { color: #ffffff; }

	p {
		color: var(--ctx-text);
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.65;
		margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
	}

	code {
		background: var(--ctx-panel);
		border: 1px solid var(--ctx-border);
		padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
		border-radius: var(--ctx-radius-sm);
		font-size: 0.9em;
		color: var(--ctx-accent-text);
		font-family: var(--ctx-font-mono);
	}

	.contact {
		background:
			radial-gradient(ellipse 60% 60% at 50% 100%, rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.06), transparent 60%),
			var(--ctx-bg);
	}

	.contact-actions {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin: 1.5rem 0;
	}

	.oss-note {
		color: var(--ctx-text-dim);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		margin-top: 1rem;
	}
</style>
